      "text": "Testing a browser game requires a comprehensive approach that goes beyond simple visual inspection, as web-based games rely heavily on client-side JavaScript and browser rendering engines. To ensure a robust and enjoyable user experience, developers must systematically test several critical components, starting with keyboard input. This involves verifying that every key press triggers the intended action without delay or error. Testers should check for key repeat issues, ensuring that holding down a movement key results in smooth, continuous motion rather than stuttering. Additionally, it is crucial to test simultaneous key presses, such as moving left while jumping, to ensure the game logic handles multiple inputs correctly without freezing or ignoring commands.\n\nCollision detection is another vital area that demands rigorous testing. Developers must verify that hitboxes align accurately with the visual sprites. This involves testing collisions with static objects, moving enemies, and collectibles. Edge cases are particularly important; for instance, testing what happens when a player collides with an object at the exact frame boundary or when multiple collisions occur simultaneously. Inaccurate collision detection can lead to frustrating gameplay, such as passing through walls or failing to register a hit.\n\nScoring systems must be validated to ensure points are awarded correctly and consistently. Testers should verify that scores update in real-time and persist correctly across game states."
